  :adr/context
  "Adding a Worker tab to cloud-itonami-app required looking at the rendered UI. The Claude-in-Chrome extension was not connected, so the native shell path was investigated as the alternative. Both the CSS and the shell turned out to have silent, pre-existing defects that no test or reviewer had caught, because in each case the failure mode renders as something that looks deliberate."
  :adr/decision
  "1. Treat an undefined CSS custom property as a build failure, not a styling preference. Add a test that cross-checks every `var(--token)` in an app's stylesheet against the tokens its design system actually defines. cloud-itonami-app now has one (`app-css-only-references-design-system-tokens-that-exist`); it was verified to fail and name the offending token when a bogus token is injected. Any repo consuming jp-go-dds should carry the same guard.

2. Do not claim a UI is verified from source inspection or from a static HTML render alone. Render it in a real engine and read back computed values. Two of the three worker state chips were transparent while the markup and CSS both read as correct.

3. A parsed-but-unconsumed flag is a defect, not a stub. `--web-url` was read and discarded for three revisions while two apps exported KOTOBA_SHELL_WEB_URL and silently got the kotoba:dom surface. Either consume an option or remove it; a flag that is accepted and ignored reads as working.

4. A commit that adds call sites must add the declarations they need. `5c39631` added uses of five members that were never declared, leaving the host uncompilable — and therefore the whole native shell unstartable — for three revisions. Build the thing you changed before landing it."
  :adr/consequences
  "The token guard makes a whole class of silent styling failure loud. It also means adding a token to an app requires the design system to define it first, which is the intended direction.

The native shell now starts and can render any app's web surface, so the workspace has a browser-extension-free way to render a page, capture it, and read back JS errors and computed styles: `kotoba-shell-host-macos-window --smoke --web-url <url> --screenshot <path>`. Smoke mode does not take activation, so it is safe to run while other sessions are working. This is the recommended way to verify a UI change on this machine.

Two apps' native paths change behaviour as a result: cloud-itonami-app and local-manimani now show their web surface where they previously showed kotoba:dom. That is what they asked for, but it is a visible change on first run after this pin advance."
  :adr/body
  "## 1. Undefined CSS custom properties are silent

An undefined custom property does not fall back through the cascade. The declaration becomes **invalid at computed-value time** and resolves to the property's initial (or inherited) value. `background: var(--nope)` therefore yields `transparent` — it does not keep a grey inherited from a base class — and `outline: 4px solid var(--nope)` yields `outline-style: none`.

cloud-itonami-app referenced **21 distinct tokens, of which 7 did not exist** in jp-go-dds (17 usages):

| referenced (nonexistent) | actual effect | replaced with |
|---|---|---|
| `--color-focus-yellow` (6x) | every `:focus-visible` computed to `outline-style:none` — **no keyboard focus ring anywhere in the app** | `--color-primitive-yellow-300` |
| `--color-success-700` (3x) | invisible text | `--color-semantic-success-2` |
| `--color-success-600` (1x) | invisible status dot | `--color-semantic-success-1` |
| `--color-success-50` (2x) | transparent chip background | `--color-primitive-green-50` |
| `--color-error-700` (2x) | invisible text | `--color-semantic-error-1` |
| `--color-error-50` (2x) | transparent chip background | `--color-primitive-red-50` |
| `--color-warning-yellow-200` (1x) | transparent chip background | `--color-primitive-yellow-200` |

jp-go-dds names semantic colors `--color-semantic-{success,error,warning-yellow}-{1,2}` and primitives `--color-primitive-{green,red,yellow,...}-{50..1200}`. **It defines no focus token at all and ships no `:focus-visible` rules of its own**, so apps must choose one; `--color-primitive-yellow-300` (#ffd43d) matches the JP-gov focus convention.

Measured before/after in WKWebView: `.state-chip--done` and `--fail` went from `rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)` to `rgb(230, 245, 236)` / `rgb(253, 238, 238)`.

Fixed in cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-app `ab2c080`.

### The same bug in tamaki-observer-web

`orgs/kotoba-lang/shell/apps/tamaki-observer-web/src/tamaki/observer/style.cljs` referenced `var(--color-focus-yellow)` in `button:focus-visible,select:focus-visible`, so no button or select had a focus ring. Confirmed against the built artifact, not just the source: `dist/style.css` contains **no** `--color-*focus*` definition at all, and `jp-go-dds.tokens/skin-css` — which `build_css.clj` concatenates after `dds.css` — does not define it either. Fixed in `658dfde` (source only; `dist/style.css` is regenerated by the shadow `:compile-finish` hook).

Two apparent extra undefined tokens there, `--stock` and `--tone`, are false positives: `--stock` is set inline at runtime (`style=\"--stock:…\"`) and `--tone` is declared as a map-string key (`{\"--tone\" \"#54f69a\"}`), which a naive `--name:` scan misses. A guard test for that repo has to allow element-scoped properties, so the cloud-itonami-app test is not directly portable to it.

## 2. The kotoba-shell native web surface never rendered — now fixed

**Resolved in kotoba-lang/shell `658dfde`.** All three defects below are fixed; the description is kept because the root cause and the contract it exposes are the useful part.

### Root cause of the build failure

`156a501` compiles with 0 errors. `5c39631` (\"rescue: the additive half of the parked shell WIP\") added call sites for `KotobaActionTarget.endpoints` / `.bodies` / `.inputValues`, `field.delegate = actionTarget`, and `retainedWindowDelegate` **without adding any of the declarations** — the rescue took the additive half literally and left the half that declares what the additions use. Every revision from `5c39631` onward fails with the same 10 errors.

The consumers determine the missing shape, so it was restored rather than guessed:

- `endpoints`/`bodies` keyed by action id, `inputValues` keyed by input id;
- `controlTextDidChange` keeps a field's text current — the only reason a field takes that object as its delegate;
- **`perform` must emit `value`**. `bin/cloud-itonami-app-action` reads `.value` as the prompt, so while `perform` omitted it the native chat button posted an empty prompt on every click. Now built through `emit()`/JSONSerialization, because the text is arbitrary and string interpolation broke the event line on any quote or newline.
- `retainedWindowDelegate` was dropped rather than declared: the top-level `let delegate` is already a strong global, so the write was redundant and declaring it would only add a written-but-never-read warning.

### Original three defects

Three stacked defects in `kotoba-lang/shell` (was `origin/main` = `d46824a`):

1. **`bin/kotoba-shell-host-macos-window.swift` does not compile** — 10 errors, e.g. `cannot find 'retainedWindowDelegate' in scope`, and `KotobaActionTarget` is missing `endpoints`/`bodies`/`inputValues` and does not conform to `NSTextFieldDelegate` while call sites use all four. Reads as a half-finished refactor. Because `bin/kotoba-shell-app-runtime` rebuilds the host inside `launch()`, **the native shell cannot start for any app**.
2. **`--web-url` is parsed and discarded** — read once at line 311, never used; `WKWebView(` appears 0 times despite `import WebKit`.
3. **The runtime never passes `--web-url`** to the host.

### The change that fixes 2 and 3

In the window host: add `--settle-seconds`; add a `writePNG(image:path:)` (the existing `writePNG(view:path:)` uses `cacheDisplay`, which **cannot capture a WKWebView** because it renders out of process — use `takeSnapshot`); inject a `WKUserScript` at `.atDocumentStart` collecting `window.onerror`/`unhandledrejection`/`console.error` into `window.__kotobaErrors` so page failures are reportable with no extension attached; add a `WKNavigationDelegate` that in smoke mode waits for `didFinish` rather than a fixed delay, then reads the errors, snapshots, emits `web/errors` + `visual/captured`, and terminates; mount a `WKWebView` as window content when `webURL != nil` (`loadFileURL` for `file:` URLs) keeping kotoba:dom as the default; and guard the existing fixed-delay smoke block with `webURL == nil`.

In smoke mode use `setActivationPolicy(.accessory)` + `.normal` window level + `orderFrontRegardless()` instead of `makeKeyAndOrderFront` + `activate(ignoringOtherApps:)`. **An observation boundary must not become the active app**: this machine runs many concurrent sessions and stealing activation corrupts whichever one is being typed into.

In `bin/kotoba-shell-app-runtime`, inside `launch()`:
`[[ -n \"${KOTOBA_SHELL_WEB_URL:-}\" ]] && host_args+=(--web-url \"$KOTOBA_SHELL_WEB_URL\")`

### Verification

Builds clean with no warnings. The kotoba:dom surface still renders and captures (regression check, since it is the default path). `--web-url` renders both a `file://` page and the live cloud-itonami-app at `http://localhost:1341/`, reporting `web/errors: []` with that app's full interaction script running — which is how the Worker surface was finally confirmed to execute without throwing against the real DOM.

## 3. `push.default = upstream` in kotoba-lang/shell sends branch pushes to main

`kotoba-lang/shell` has `push.default = upstream` in its git config. A branch created with `git worktree add -b <name> origin/main` therefore has `origin/main` as its upstream, and **`git push origin <name>` pushes the commit to `main`, not to `refs/heads/<name>`**. It reports `Everything up-to-date` afterwards while `gh api .../merges` fails with `Head does not exist`, because the branch was never created remotely.

`658dfde` landed on main this way — a clean fast-forward, no force-push and no history rewrite, but not the intended feature-branch-plus-server-side-merge route. Anyone intending a reviewable branch in this repo must push an explicit refspec: `git push origin <name>:refs/heads/<name>`."
